    "answer": "Based on the provided text, Pemex creates obligations and potential debt additions for Mexico's public finances through:\n\n- **Financial support operations and pre-capitalized notes:** Financial operations have been undertaken to support Pemex, and the authorities and IMF staff are discussing the statistical treatment of pre-capitalized notes (amounting to about 0.6 percent of GDP), which are currently excluded from gross public debt numbers.\n- **Supplier debt repayment:** Upcoming repayment of supplier debt through the newly created Pemex Investment Fund may entail an increase in the Debt Sustainability Analysis (DSA) stock of gross public debt, because Pemex's financial obligations are part of the DSA debt perimeter, whereas accounts payable (including supplier debt) currently are not.\n- **Supplier debt arrears:** Arrears on Pemex's supplier debt—which are not included in the published net or gross stock of public sector borrowing requirements—stood at 0.6 percent of GDP at end-2024 and 0.1 percent of GDP as of September 12, 2025.",
